{"name":"Blue Chip","hex":"#1d5699","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue chip","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A high-value poker chip.","A stock or other investment that has a high price because of its perceived reliability."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue_chip"}],"entries":[{"title":"Casino chip","blocks":[{"text":"Casino chips are small discs used as currency in casinos. Larger, rectangular gaming plaques may be used for high-stakes games. Poker chips are also widely used as play money in casual or tournament games, are of numismatic value to casino chip collectors, or may be kept as souvenirs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/IsthmusCityChips.jpg/330px-IsthmusCityChips.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casino_chip"},{"title":"Blue Chip Casino, Hotel and Spa","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Chip Casino, Hotel and Spa is a riverboat casino located in Michigan City, Indiana. It is owned by Boyd Gaming."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Blue_Chip_Casino%2C_Hotel_and_Resort.jpg/330px-Blue_Chip_Casino%2C_Hotel_and_Resort.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Chip_Casino%2C_Hotel_and_Spa"},{"title":"Blue chip (sports)","blocks":[{"text":"Blue chip is a term used in the United States to describe athletes, particularly high school players, targeted for drafting or signing by teams at the college level. In college football, the term is considered synonymous with four-star and five-star recruits, while in college basketball, the term may also refer exclusively to five-stars. Collegiate players being scouted by professional franchises may also be referred to as blue chips."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_chip_(sports)"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/MDGGamingPlaque.jpg/250px-MDGGamingPlaque.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/MDGGamingPlaque.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/MDGGamingPlaque.jpg/250px-MDGGamingPlaque.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"50,000 Malagasy franc gaming plaque from Grand Cercle casino, Antananarivo, Madagascar, circa 1995.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMDGGamingPlaque.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Virapulla_-_IMG_7659.jpg/500px-Virapulla_-_IMG_7659.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Virapulla_-_IMG_7659.jpg/1280px-Virapulla_-_IMG_7659.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Virapulla_-_IMG